1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,670
This counter misses its 250 MHz target.

2
00:00:04,310 --> 00:00:07,130
The slowest path ends at a register’s clock
enable.

3
00:00:07,730 --> 00:00:12,090
The decision to update the count arrives 633
picoseconds late.

4
00:00:12,830 --> 00:00:15,870
In TimingLens, open the guided counter
experiment.

5
00:00:16,270 --> 00:00:19,590
It includes the original source, the proposed
change,

6
00:00:19,590 --> 00:00:21,830
and the 4-nanosecond clock constraint.

7
00:00:22,470 --> 00:00:23,550
Here’s the line we’re testing.

8
00:00:24,170 --> 00:00:27,630
Before the counter can subtract, it checks
whether there’s enough left.

9
00:00:27,630 --> 00:00:30,530
The remaining count is 32 bits wide.

10
00:00:31,010 --> 00:00:35,390
The amount to subtract is only 4 bits, so the
largest request is 15.

11
00:00:35,770 --> 00:00:36,930
That gives us a shortcut.

12
00:00:37,510 --> 00:00:41,610
If any of the upper 28 bits is set, at least
16 remain.

13
00:00:42,110 --> 00:00:43,970
Even a request for 15 fits.

14
00:00:44,470 --> 00:00:48,370
If all those bits are zero, the low 4 bits
tell the whole story.

15
00:00:48,810 --> 00:00:51,770
With 9 remaining, a request for 12 must wait.

16
00:00:52,350 --> 00:00:54,830
The proposed change combines those two
checks.

17
00:00:54,830 --> 00:00:58,530
It keeps the same subtraction and adds no
clock cycle.

18
00:00:59,350 --> 00:01:01,110
Select Validate and run experiment.

19
00:01:01,850 --> 00:01:06,790
Both versions get the same inputs for 10,289
simulated cycles.

20
00:01:07,210 --> 00:01:10,270
Then Vivado places and routes isolated copies

21
00:01:10,270 --> 00:01:13,250
with the same clock and the same
implementation recipe.

22
00:01:13,670 --> 00:01:15,290
We’re speeding through those builds here.

23
00:01:15,870 --> 00:01:18,630
The Experiment Lab opens on the routed
results.

24
00:01:18,630 --> 00:01:22,730
Worst setup slack goes from minus 0.633

25
00:01:22,730 --> 00:01:26,550
to plus 0.407 nanoseconds.

26
00:01:26,690 --> 00:01:29,710
All 32 failing setup endpoints now pass.

27
00:01:30,310 --> 00:01:33,230
The core also uses 9 fewer lookup tables.

28
00:01:33,670 --> 00:01:37,520
This standalone counter meets timing at 250
MHz.

29
00:01:38,090 --> 00:01:40,510
The next check is timing in the full system.
